.Dd September 23, 2026
.Dt ICOWM-SESSION 5
.Os IcoWM 1.0.1
.Sh NAME
.Nm session.json
.Nd session lifecycle hooks for IcoWM
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Pa session.json
lists commands
.Xr icowm 1
launches at three points in a session's life.
The file is optional: absent or malformed, no hooks run at all.
.Pp
Each command is expanded with
.Xr wordexp 3
semantics and then started through
.Xr fork 2
and
.Xr execvp 3 .
A hook runs independently of the window manager, which only logs that it
started and how it ended.
Command substitution is disabled, and environment-variable expansion is
disabled as well wherever
.Dv WRDE_NOENV
is available.
.Pp
Every key holds an array of strings, empty by default.
Only non-empty strings are used; anything else in the array is ignored.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic on-start
Run once startup initialization has finished.
.It Ic on-reload
Run after a configuration reload, whether from
.Dv SIGHUP
or from the reload action.
.It Ic on-exit
Run as
.Xr icowm 1
is exiting.
.El
.Pp
Note that the emergency exit shortcut skips
.Ic on-exit
entirely, along with everything else the ordinary quit action does.
That is the point of it, for it exists for a session that may already
be broken, so it takes the shortest path out.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME}/icowm/session.json
The file this page describes, at the first of the several locations
.Xr icowm 1
looks in.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
A session that dresses the desktop when it starts, says so when the
configuration is reloaded, and cleans up after itself when it ends:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{
    "on-start": [
        "feh --bg-fill ~/.background.png",
        "dex --autostart",
        "nm-applet",
        "picom --daemon"
    ],
    "on-reload": [
        "notify-send -i configuration 'IcoWM' 'Configuration reloaded'"
    ],
    "on-exit": [
        "pkill picom"
    ]
}
.Ed
.Pp
The four commands at start-up set a wallpaper, with whatever tool is
at hand, run the autostart entries the desktop-entry specification
puts in
.Pa ~/.config/autostart ,
bring up a network tray applet, and start a compositor.
A solid background color needs none of this, being what
.Ic desktop.color.background
already sets; an image is the part no theme can do, which is why
a hook is the place for it.
Only the compositor is stopped again on the way out, the rest ending
with the session that owns them.
